`POST /invoices/<id>/pay` is in and committed on `feature/pay-receipts`. All 10 tests passed against this worktree's services (the 5 new ones included). I then ran `stack down`, which stopped this worktree's four services. I didn't touch any other worktree's services.

- **Paying:** an open invoice flips to `paid` in a single atomic update, so two concurrent pay requests can't both succeed. It then clears the cached copy and queues an `issue_receipt` job. An already-paid invoice returns 409 and queues nothing. An unknown invoice returns 404. `GET /invoices/<id>` shows `paid` straight after, even if the invoice was cached.
- **Duplicate jobs:** I added a migration, `migrations/20261118_receipts_unique_invoice.sql`, that adds a unique index on `receipts.invoice_id`. It first deletes any existing duplicate receipts, keeping the lowest id for each invoice. The worker inserts with `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`, so a redelivered job records nothing the second time. The worker also skips invoices that aren't `paid`.
- **Tests:** the new tests in `tests/test_api.py` cover:
  - paying a cached invoice and getting one receipt;
  - paying twice returns 409 with nothing queued;
  - an unknown invoice returns 404;
  - three redelivered jobs for one invoice still leave a single receipt;
  - the new migration is applied.

The code changes are in `app.py`, `ledger/db.py` and `ledger/jobs.py`.

I left `PATCH /invoices/<id>` alone. It doesn't clear the cache, and it rewrites the whole row, including `status`. A PATCH that runs at the same moment as a pay could set the status back to `open`. That is outside this task.
